I would advise **neither to withdraw at once nor to stay as things now are**, but to reduce participation temporarily and at the same time change its manner. Run a trial for **6-8 weeks**: either every second Thursday night shift, or less frequent night shifts with more work by day or on preparation and administration. In this way you will preserve the useful service, and you will see more clearly what is happening in you.

The boasts about calmly recounting things, when done with difficulty, do not by themselves prove vice; for it can be both real experience and composure. Mixed impulses often occur in people. It would be a loss if you practiced seeming to suffer nothing more than becoming prudent and steady. For steadiness appears not only in endurance toward danger, but also in sleep, rest, gentleness toward family, and in being able to say: "I cannot now."

Therefore set down heavily what is said by Tamar, not as an accusation, but as testimony about the fruit of the practice. Before the trial write a few things you will observe:

- how much and how you sleep after shifts and actual outings;
- how much time you need before recovering;
- whether you become sharper or more impatient toward Uriel and Tamar;
- whether before the shift you are driven by anticipation or by fear and aversion;
- whether you can miss one shift without shame, blame, or fear that you will lose honor.

Also speak with Carmen and Gideon before changing, making a clear period and handover of duties; for no one abandons colleagues when, having forewarned, he takes care to have others fill the gap. Say that you wish to remain useful, but the continuous night shifts harm the household, and for this reason you are testing a moderately measured participation. And in the group do not compete over who will recount the most horrifying things; after difficult events seek a common brief examination of what happened, what was done well, and what rest or help each person needs.

If then in the trial sleep, gentleness, and the household improve, and you are able to seek to be useful without being praised, stay in this more moderate manner. But if the same things remain, or the group does not allow reasonable boundaries and praises the harm as courage, complete withdrawal is not shameful. But if sleeplessness, disturbing memories, continuous hyperarousal, or danger from fatigue occur, do not wait, but reduce or stop and seek professional help.

For the steadiest person is not the one who always endures everything, but the one who also guards the boundaries of the body and the duties toward family. Withdrawal from uninterrupted shift work is not withdrawal from the good, especially if you preserve another useful contribution.

